{:nav-title "Find specs"
 :content
 [:div
  [:h1 "Find Specifications"]
  [:p "Every query so far has used the default " [:code ":find"] " form, "
   "which returns a " [:strong "collection of tuples"] ". "
   "Datomic Datalog offers four distinct find specifications, each returning a different shape. "
   "Choosing the right one makes downstream code simpler."]

  [:h2 "Relation (default) — collection of tuples"]
  [:p "The standard form. Each variable becomes a column; each matching combination becomes a row. "
   "Order among the rows is not guaranteed."]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name ?speed
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [?e :stat/speed ?speed]
    [(> ?speed 100)]]]

  [:h2 "Collection — flat vector of one variable"]
  [:p "Wrap a single variable in " [:code "[?var ...]"] " to get a flat vector of its values "
   "instead of a collection of single-element tuples. "
   "Useful when you only care about one column and want to pass it directly to other code:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find [?name ...]
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [?e :stat/speed ?speed]
    [(> ?speed 100)]]]

  [:h2 "Scalar — single value"]
  [:p "Append " [:code "."] " after the variable to return exactly one value instead of a collection. "
   "When multiple rows match, which one you get is arbitrary, "
   "so scalars are most useful with aggregations that already produce a single row, "
   "like " [:code "(max ...)"] ":"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find (max ?speed) .
    :where
    [?e :stat/speed ?speed]]]
  [:p "Without the " [:code "."] " this would return " [:code "[[150]]"] ": "
   "a collection containing one single-element tuple. "
   "With it you get the bare number " [:code "150"] "."]

  [:h2 "Tuple — single row"]
  [:p "Wrap multiple variables in " [:code "[?a ?b]"] " (no " [:code "..."] ") "
   "to return exactly one tuple. "
   "If the query matches several rows, you get one of them and Datomic does not tell you the others existed, "
   "so the query has to be guaranteed to match one row. That takes two things: "
   "the lookup goes through a " [:strong "unique attribute"] ", and every other attribute it joins "
   "is " [:strong "cardinality one"] ". "
   "In this database " [:code ":pokemon/number"] " is declared " [:code ":db.unique/identity"] ", "
   "and " [:code ":pokemon/name"] ", " [:code ":stat/hp"] " and " [:code ":stat/speed"] " each hold one value per Pokemon:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find [?name ?hp ?speed]
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/number "025"]
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [?e :stat/hp ?hp]
    [?e :stat/speed ?speed]]]
  [:p "The result is a plain vector " [:code "[\"Pikachu\" 35 90]"] " "
   "rather than a collection of tuples, easy to destructure directly in Clojure. "
   "Any number that exists in the database gives exactly one row."]

  [:h3 "What could go wrong"]
  [:p "Break either condition and the tuple spec stops being safe. "
   "This query looks the Pokemon up by " [:code ":pokemon/name"] ", which the schema does not make unique, "
   "and it joins " [:code ":pokemon/type"] ", which is cardinality many. "
   "Bulbasaur is Grass and Poison, so "
   [:ui/value "pokemon" [:find (count ?type) . :where [?e :pokemon/name "Bulbasaur"] [?e :pokemon/type ?type]]]
   " rows match. Before you run it, predict what the tuple spec returns:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find [?number ?type ?speed]
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name "Bulbasaur"]
    [?e :pokemon/number ?number]
    [?e :pokemon/type ?type]
    [?e :stat/speed ?speed]]]
  [:p "You get a single tuple with one of the two types. There is no error and no warning, "
   "and the other type is gone. Swap in \"Pikachu\" and the same query looks fine, "
   "but only because Pikachu happens to have one type."]
  [:p "When you want one entity with everything it has, use " [:code "pull"] " with the scalar find spec instead. "
   "The Pull chapter covers it: a cardinality-many attribute comes back as a collection holding every value."]
  [:ui/try-tip
   [:p "Look up other Pokemon by number in the safe query, such as \"150\" or \"143\". "
    "Then change the name in the Bulbasaur query to \"Charizard\" or \"Pikachu\" and predict the result each time. "
    "Or combine the scalar spec with " [:code "(min ?speed)"] " to return just the single lowest speed value in the Pokedex."]]

  [:details
   [:summary [:strong "Under the hood: Peer API vs Client API"]]
   [:p "Datomic has two Clojure APIs for running queries: the " [:strong "Peer API"]
    " (" [:code "datomic.api"] ") and the " [:strong "Client API"]
    " (" [:code "datomic.client.api"] "). They handle find specs differently, "
    "so it helps to know which one your own code uses."]
   [:ul
    [:li [:strong "Peer API: "] "all four find specs work as described above, "
     "and a relation find returns a " [:em "set"] " of tuples."]
    [:li [:strong "Client API: "] "only relation finds are accepted, and they return a "
     [:em "vector"] " of tuples instead of a set. To get the other shapes, reshape the result yourself: "
     [:code "(mapv first result)"] " for a collection, "
     [:code "(ffirst result)"] " for a scalar, "
     [:code "(first result)"] " for a tuple."]]
   [:p "This site runs on Datomic Local, which uses the Client API. "
    "So you can still try every form above, the server rewrites each query into a relation find "
    "and reshapes the result to match what the Peer API would give you. "
    "The one visible difference is the relation example at the top of this chapter, which prints as a vector "
    "where the Peer API would print a set."]]]}
